          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often does guidance describe checking thyroid function once treatment is stable?",
          "answer": "Once treatment for hypothyroidism is stable, guidance from the Endocrinology and Thyroid Association suggests that thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels should be checked at least once a year. This is in addition to checking TSH six to eight weeks after starting levothyroxine or after any dose change, since it takes time to adjust to a new steady state. Regular monitoring helps ensure that hormone levels remain within the target range. \n\nFor further details and to confirm this recommendation, refer to the document titled “Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change” from the Endocrinology and Thyroid Association.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
